      "why": "The yellow switch at R3C3 is free on the first move and looks like the answer: it reverses the red block at R5C5, which is locked head-to-head with the green block at R5C4 and can only leave once it is reversed. But the same switch also reverses the green block at R5C2, and reversed it would face the blue block at R6C2 head-to-head for good. So the switch must wait until that green block has left upward - through the chain gate at R4C2, which opens only when its one link, the purple spinner at R3C4, has left. That spinner can only leave once it is turned to face right, by the block beside it (P at R3C5) leaving; the free spinner P at R2C4 above it turns it too, at the wrong moment. Order: turn the gate's link with P at R3C5 and let it out to the right, send green up through the open gate, and only then fire the switch. Afterwards the bottom row still needs care: the yellow spinner at R5C3 must be turned twice (by G at R5C4 and the counter-clockwise spinner G at R6C3) before it can leave. Checked over every winning line (30): the first four moves are always the same - P at R3C5, the gate's link, green up, the switch - so only 1 of the 3 legal first moves keeps the board solvable.",
